# Stripe webhook IP allowlist — PRODUCTION ONLY, not enabled by default.
#
# This is the edge/nginx shape of the control, for a self-managed host
# (Forge or similar) that has an nginx config to add it to. The currently
# deployed target (Railway/Railpack/Caddy, ADR-0024) has no such file, so it
# uses App\Http\Middleware\RestrictStripeWebhookIps instead — same check,
# same source list, done in PHP against $request->ip() once trustProxies
# has resolved it. Read that class's docblock before assuming this file is
# the only way to get this protection.
#
# Stripe's guidance is to use BOTH protections, not either:
#
#   "IP allowlisting: Stripe sends webhook events from a set list of IP
#    addresses. Configure your server or firewall to only accept webhook
#    requests from these addresses.
#    Signature verification: ..."
#
# The application already does signature verification unconditionally
# (App\Http\Middleware\VerifyStripeWebhookSignature). This file is the other
# half, and it lives at the edge because that is where it belongs — nginx
# can refuse the connection before PHP is ever started, which is the point.
#
# ── Why this is NOT enabled in local development ──────────────────────────
#
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8080/stripe/webhook` forwards events
# from the CLI running on your own machine, so they arrive from a Docker
# bridge address, never from Stripe's published ranges. Turning this on
# locally would reject every forwarded event and look exactly like a broken
# signature. Enable it only where Stripe connects to you directly.
#
# ── How to enable it in production ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Fetch the CURRENT list. Do not trust the addresses below to still be
#    right — Stripe changes them, and a stale allowlist silently drops real
#    payments:
#
#        curl https://stripe.com/files/ips/ips_webhooks.txt
#
#    The canonical page is https://docs.stripe.com/ips
#
# 2. Regenerate the `allow` lines from that output.
# 3. Include this file inside the webhook location block in default.conf:
#
#        location = /stripe/webhook {
#            include /etc/nginx/conf.d/stripe-ip-allowlist.conf;
#            try_files $uri /index.php?$query_string;
#        }
#
# 4. If you terminate TLS at a load balancer or CDN, `$remote_addr` is the
#    proxy, not Stripe — every request would be denied. Set up
#    `real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For` and `set_real_ip_from <proxy range>`
#    first, and verify with a real delivery before relying on it.
#
# 5. Re-check the list on a schedule. This is the failure mode that hurts:
#    it works for months, Stripe adds a range, and a fraction of your
#    webhooks start 403ing with nothing in the application logs.
#
# ── Snapshot, 2026-09-01 — VERIFY BEFORE USE ──────────────────────────────
#
# These were current when this file was written and are here as a shape to
# copy, not a list to trust.

allow 3.18.12.63;
allow 3.130.192.231;
allow 13.235.14.237;
allow 13.235.122.149;
allow 18.211.135.69;
allow 35.154.171.200;
allow 52.15.183.38;
allow 54.88.130.119;
allow 54.88.130.237;
allow 54.187.174.169;
allow 54.187.205.235;
allow 54.187.216.72;

deny all;
